<DocsLayout
  title="GCP security collector"
  description="What om scan gcp collects from Compute Engine, Cloud Storage IAM, and service accounts, and which edges those resources produce."
>
  <h1>GCP security collector</h1>
  <p>
    <code>om scan gcp</code> requires <code>GCP_PROJECT_ID</code>. Clients use Application Default
    Credentials (<code>gcloud auth application-default login</code>,
    <code>GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS</code>, or the metadata server). The Compute client
    requests the cloud-platform scope. One scan covers one project. Firewall rules, VPC networks,
    Cloud SQL, and project IAM bindings are not inventoried.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`export GCP_PROJECT_ID=my-project
gcloud auth application-default login
./om scan gcp
./om rules run`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>GCP_REGION</code> (default <code>us-central1</code>) is the region segment on service
    account node ids and on the id of each bucket. Instance ids use the zone from the aggregated
    list. The bucket node’s <code>region</code> field is the bucket location, which can differ
    from the id segment.
  </p>

  <h2>Resources</h2>
  <div class="docs-table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>API</th>
          <th>Graph</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Compute <code>instances.aggregatedList</code></td>
          <td><code>Workload</code>. Properties: <code>resource_id</code> (self link), <code>public_ip</code> (NAT IP, or empty), <code>status</code>. A NAT IP adds <code>REACHABLE</code> from <code>internet:global</code>. Firewall rules are not consulted.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Storage bucket list, then each bucket’s IAM policy</td>
          <td><code>Datastore</code> with <code>resource_id</code> (bucket name) and <code>public_access</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>IAM service accounts list</td>
          <td><code>Identity</code> named by email, with <code>email</code> and <code>admin_access</code>.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Node ids are <code>{'gcp:{project}:{zone-or-region}:{kind}:{resource}'}</code>. Instance
    resources use the instance name. Service account resources use <code>uniqueId</code>, so the
    displayed name (the email) can change without a new id. A bucket IAM policy that cannot be
    read is treated as not public. The scan continues.
  </p>

  <h2>When a bucket is public</h2>
  <p>
    <code>public_access</code> is true when the bucket IAM policy grants
    <code>allUsers</code> or <code>allAuthenticatedUsers</code> one of these roles:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>roles/storage.admin</code>, <code>roles/storage.objectAdmin</code>, <code>roles/storage.objectViewer</code></li>
    <li>legacy bucket owner, legacy object owner, legacy object reader</li>
    <li>primitive owner, editor, or viewer</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Public access prevention is not read. A bucket can have prevention unset and still be stored
    as private when those members are absent. <code>allUsers</code> on a role that cannot read
    objects, such as <code>roles/storage.legacyBucketReader</code>, does not set
    <code>public_access</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Service accounts</h2>
  <p>
    <code>admin_access</code> is true when the display name or email contains <code>admin</code>,
    case-insensitively. Project IAM bindings are not expanded, and Workload Identity is not
    mapped onto GCE instances. A service account named <code>admin</code> looks administrative
    even when its roles are narrow, and a highly privileged account whose email does not contain
    <code>admin</code> does not.
  </p>

  <h2>Heuristic edges</h2>
  <p>
    The same post-pass as Azure applies inside this project:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>CAN_ACCESS</code> from each service account with <code>admin_access: true</code> to every public bucket.</li>
    <li><code>CAN_ACCESS</code> from each instance that has a <code>REACHABLE</code> edge to those same buckets.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There is no binding between an instance and the service account it runs as, so blast radius
    from a service account does not include the VMs that use it unless some other edge says so.
    Private buckets receive no <code>CAN_ACCESS</code> edge from this collector.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
